# Documentation Conventions

## File Naming
`UPPERCASE_WITH_UNDERSCORES.md` — e.g. `BASIC_AUTH.md`, `ASSET_MERGING.md`

## Standard Structure
Each feature doc follows this order:

```markdown
# Feature Name

One-line description of what it does.

## Quick Start
Minimal working code — no setup noise.

## API
Grouped function signatures only (no prose).

## Example: Specific Use Case
Full working code with inline comments.

## Troubleshooting  (if relevant)
Short problem → solution pairs.

## Security / Notes  (if relevant)
Warnings last, not first.
```

## Quick Start — Keep It Minimal
```cpp
// ✅ GOOD — shows the 3 lines needed to enable the feature
server.setBasicAuth(true);
server.addBasicAuthUser("admin", "secret123");
server.addProtectedPage("/admin");
```

## API Section — Signatures Only
Group by category with a comment label, no implementation details:
```cpp
// Enable/disable
void setBasicAuth(bool enabled);
void enableBasicAuth();

// User management
void addBasicAuthUser(const std::string& username, const std::string& password);
bool removeBasicAuthUser(const std::string& username);
```

## Code Blocks
- Always use `cpp`, `bash`, `cmake`, `json`, or `nginx` language tags
- Inline explanations with `// comment`, not surrounding prose
- Raw string literals for JSON bodies: `R"({"key":"value"})"`

## Cross-References
Link to related docs at the end of sections:
```markdown
See [ASSET_MERGING.md](ASSET_MERGING.md) for complete documentation.
```

## Warnings
Use `**⚠️ ...**` for security or breaking behaviour — place at the end of the doc, not the top.

## Tone
- Direct and technical — no filler phrases
- Present tense: "Serves files" not "Will serve files"
- No emojis in body text (only `⚠️` for warnings)
